"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,040\nThe Learning Brain.\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,549\nEpisode five.\n\n3\n00:00:07,569 --> 00:00:09,981\nFocused and diffuse modes.\n\n4\n00:00:12,967 --> 00:00:15,261\nYou've been stuck on a problem for an hour.\n\n5\n00:00:15,548 --> 00:00:16,294\nYou give up,\n\n6\n00:00:16,366 --> 00:00:17,168\ngo for a walk,\n\n7\n00:00:17,188 --> 00:00:18,683\nand halfway down the block,\n\n8\n00:00:18,703 --> 00:00:20,082\nthe answer shows up.\n\n9\n00:00:20,426 --> 00:00:21,629\nWhy does that happen?\n\n10\n00:00:21,699 --> 00:00:24,790\nOne popular way to describe it uses two modes.\n\n11\n00:00:24,998 --> 00:00:27,342\nFocused mode is tight and effortful.\n\n12\n00:00:27,521 --> 00:00:29,283\nIt follows familiar paths,\n\n13\n00:00:29,303 --> 00:00:29,633\nfast,\n\n14\n00:00:29,653 --> 00:00:31,017\nbut only nearby.\n\n15\n00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:33,847\nDiffuse mode is relaxed and wide.\n\n16\n00:00:34,207 --> 00:00:35,164\nIt makes long,\n\n17\n00:00:35,184 --> 00:00:37,735\nloose jumps between distant ideas.\n\n18\n00:00:37,986 --> 00:00:39,548\nThis is a teaching metaphor,\n\n19\n00:00:39,568 --> 00:00:41,270\nfrom Barbara Oakley's work,\n\n20\n00:00:41,409 --> 00:00:42,590\nnot a brain map.\n\n21\n00:00:43,006 --> 00:00:45,460\nBut it's loosely tied to something real.\n\n22\n00:00:45,530 --> 00:00:47,349\nDuring demanding tasks,\n\n23\n00:00:47,398 --> 00:00:49,694\na set of attention regions switches on.\n\n24\n00:00:50,141 --> 00:00:50,996\nAt rest,\n\n25\n00:00:51,073 --> 00:00:52,076\na different set,\n\n26\n00:00:52,096 --> 00:00:53,473\nthe default mode network,\n\n27\n00:00:53,493 --> 00:00:54,855\nbecomes more active,\n\n28\n00:00:55,099 --> 00:00:58,084\nand the two tend to rise and fall in opposition.\n\n29\n00:00:58,550 --> 00:01:01,823\nThe default network is busy when the mind wanders,\n\n30\n00:01:01,870 --> 00:01:03,404\nremembers,\n\n31\n00:01:03,424 --> 00:01:03,839\nand imagines.\n\n32\n00:01:03,909 --> 00:01:05,869\nDoes stepping away actually help?\n\n33\n00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:09,613\nA review of more than a hundred studies found that it does,\n\n34\n00:01:09,633 --> 00:01:10,572\nmodestly.\n\n35\n00:01:10,978 --> 00:01:13,129\nBreaks worked best after a long,\n\n36\n00:01:13,149 --> 00:01:14,267\nreal attempt,\n\n37\n00:01:14,417 --> 00:01:17,152\nand when the break was filled with something easy,\n\n38\n00:01:17,185 --> 00:01:18,785\nnot something demanding.\n\n39\n00:01:18,855 --> 00:01:20,309\nSleep may help too.\n\n40\n00:01:20,522 --> 00:01:21,227\nIn one study,\n\n41\n00:01:21,247 --> 00:01:24,423\npeople practiced a number task with a hidden shortcut.\n\n42\n00:01:24,798 --> 00:01:26,074\nAfter a night's sleep,\n\n43\n00:01:26,101 --> 00:01:28,427\nfifty-nine percent found the shortcut.\n\n44\n00:01:28,774 --> 00:01:30,626\nIn the groups that stayed awake,\n\n45\n00:01:30,649 --> 00:01:32,571\nno more than a quarter did.\n\n46\n00:01:32,641 --> 00:01:33,842\nTwo cautions.\n\n47\n00:01:34,063 --> 00:01:37,015\nSome later sleep studies found smaller effects,\n\n48\n00:01:37,035 --> 00:01:37,815\nor none.\n\n49\n00:01:38,058 --> 00:01:41,532\nAnd creative thinking doesn't simply flip between modes.\n\n50\n00:01:41,756 --> 00:01:45,330\nThe default and control networks often work together during it.\n\n51\n00:01:45,717 --> 00:01:48,821\nTreat focused and diffuse as a useful picture,\n\n52\n00:01:48,922 --> 00:01:50,251\nnot a mechanism.\n\n53\n00:01:50,321 --> 00:01:50,957\nFor teaching,\n\n54\n00:01:50,977 --> 00:01:52,655\nthe message is about rhythm.\n\n55\n00:01:52,946 --> 00:01:55,292\nGive students a hard problem early.\n\n56\n00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:57,403\nLet them work on it in earnest.\n\n57\n00:01:57,658 --> 00:01:58,904\nThen build in a gap,\n\n58\n00:01:58,924 --> 00:01:59,681\na break,\n\n59\n00:01:59,725 --> 00:02:00,137\na night,\n\n60\n00:02:00,157 --> 00:02:01,193\nor a different task,\n\n61\n00:02:01,213 --> 00:02:02,903\nbefore you come back to it.\n\n62\n00:02:03,125 --> 00:02:04,541\nAnd tell them plainly.\n\n63\n00:02:04,808 --> 00:02:07,394\nBeing stuck is not the same as failing.\n\n64\n00:02:07,464 --> 00:02:09,224\nFocus loads the problem.\n\n65\n00:02:09,345 --> 00:02:11,685\nStepping away lets it reorganize.\n\n66\n00:02:11,754 --> 00:02:13,361\nLearning needs both.\n"
